 # Design before you write code
 
-One attempt at a hard design locks in the first shape the model thought of. `/architect` settles types and boundaries before implementation. `/arena` runs several attempts at the same brief and merges the best parts. `/interrogate` has other models try to break the result. When the job is coverage rather than design synthesis, `/swarm` fans out slices or races and aggregates their results.
+One attempt at a hard design locks in the first shape the model thought of. `/istack:architect` settles types and boundaries before implementation. `/istack:arena` runs several attempts at the same brief and merges the best parts. `/istack:interrogate` has other models try to break the result. When the job is coverage rather than design synthesis, `/istack:swarm` fans out slices or races and aggregates their results.
 
 The two most common design mistakes are taking the agent's first design and polishing a plan that no code has tested. This page fixes both. You plan through code. Prototypes answer the open questions, a README or tutorial sets the target, and the written plan comes last.
 
+Every fan-out on this page runs the same way. Each worker is an agent in its own Herdr terminal, started by `istack-worker`. One worker per seat of the role, so a mixed panel means mixed model families. You watch them in Herdr's sidebar and steer one with `hc send <name> "<text>"`. The [workers doc](../../docs/workers.md) has the mechanics.
+
